# Make org.freedesktop.secrets D-Bus activatable.
#
# ksecretd.service keeps this name owned for the whole Hyprland session, so on
# paper this file is redundant. It is not, and 1Password is the proof: it failed
# with
#
#   org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.ServiceUnknown — "The name is not activatable"
#
# having probed the keyring during its own startup, four minutes before
# ksecretd.service came up. It cached that result and kept reusing the dead path
# for the next nine hours, long after the name was owned.
#
# A unit answers "is it running right now"; an activation file answers "can it
# be started on demand", and those are different questions. Any client that
# starts before the unit, races it, or arrives after a crash asks the second
# one — and the packaged
# /usr/share/dbus-1/services/org.kde.secretservicecompat.service registers
# ksecretd under its KDE name only, so the freedesktop name had no answer.
#
# SystemdService= rather than a bare Exec=: activation is routed through the
# unit so there is exactly one ksecretd. Handing the bus its own Exec= line
# instead produces a second, systemd-invisible instance that races the unit for
# the name — which is a worse bug than the one being fixed, and harder to see.
# The unit is Type=dbus/BusName=org.freedesktop.secrets, so systemd treats the
# name appearing as the unit having started, and the two agree.
#
# Note the bus reads this directory once at startup: a freshly-deployed file
# needs `busctl --user call org.freedesktop.DBus /org/freedesktop/DBus
# org.freedesktop.DBus ReloadConfig` (or a new session) before it takes effect.
# 26-hyprland-session.sh does that reload on every apply.

[D-BUS Service]
Name=org.freedesktop.secrets
Exec=/usr/bin/ksecretd
SystemdService=ksecretd.service
